---
import Section from './Section.astro';

const INDEX_BYTES = 362121;
const ENGINE_BYTES = 2163;
const SRAM_BUDGET = 65536;

const share = (value: number) => `${((value / INDEX_BYTES) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`;

const bars = [
  {
    label: 'index on',
    bytes: INDEX_BYTES,
    value: '~354 KB',
    fill: 'bar__fill--index',
  },
  {
    label: '64 KB SRAM budget',
    bytes: SRAM_BUDGET,
    value: '65,536 B',
    fill: 'bar__fill--budget',
  },
  {
    label: 'index off',
    bytes: ENGINE_BYTES,
    value: '2,163 B',
    fill: 'bar__fill--engine',
  },
];

const measurements = [
  { what: 'Live peak heap, two facts and a read back', value: '2,163 bytes' },
  { what: 'Static SRAM used, inside a 64 KB budget', value: 'about 32 KB' },
  { what: 'Firmware text (flash)', value: 'about 121 KB' },
];

const latency = [
  { scale: '100,000 records', indexed: '25.4 to 26.3 µs', scan: '915 to 941 µs' },
  { scale: '1,000,000 records', indexed: '226.6 to 229.5 µs', scan: '18.6 to 19.4 ms' },
];
---

<Section
  id="proof"
  label="Proof"
  title="Running without an operating system is a measurement"
  lede="A firmware image boots in emulation on a RISC-V target with no operating system and no runtime, completes a write and a read, and peaks at 2,163 bytes of live heap."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The build enforces a 64 KB SRAM budget on the constrained path. A second build compiles the
      C surface and runs the same test. Both run in continuous integration on every change.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="bars">
    {
      bars.map((bar) => (
        <div class:list={['bar', bar.label === 'index off' && 'bar--engine']}>
          <span class="bar__label">{bar.label}</span>
          <span class="bar__track">
            <span class:list={['bar__fill', bar.fill]} style={`width:${share(bar.bytes)}`} />
          </span>
          <span class="bar__value">{bar.value}</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    Published benchmark. Live peak heap for the MCU round trip with the structural index on and off,
    on one scale. The optional index costs about 354 KB of live heap, which is why the reference
    build leaves it off and keeps the engine in the 64 KB class. The reproduction conditions are
    given below.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measurement</th>
          <th>Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          measurements.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="lead">{row.what}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.value}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h3>Three-axis query latency</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A three-axis query over a one-day window answers in about 0.93 ms at 100,000 records and
        about 18.9 ms at one million, from the same structure that holds every other axis, and
        the cost grows with the number of records.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Scale</th>
          <th>Structural index</th>
          <th>Scan path</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          latency.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="lead">{row.scale}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.indexed}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.scan}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    Published benchmark. Those figures come from a virtual test environment, over volumes of a
    hundred thousand records and up, and are the median of ten samples from the Criterion harness on
    a release build. The scan path is the one the read path uses, and the device runs it over the
    records it holds; the on-target cost is not yet measured.
  </p>
</Section>

<style>
  .bar--engine .bar__label {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
